---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Star from "./Star.astro";
import archedDoors from "../assets/heritage/arched-doors.jpg";
---

<section class="relative overflow-hidden bg-navy text-paper">
  {/* Heritage arched doors as a texture band, held well back under a strong
      navy overlay so the belief statement stays fully readable. */}
  <Image
    src={archedDoors}
    alt="Row of carved arched doors in a Bahraini heritage house"
    widths={[768, 1280, 1920]}
    sizes="100vw"
    loading="lazy"
    class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover"
  />
  <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-navy/85 naqsh-light"></div>

  <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-24 text-center">
    <Star tone="brass" class="mx-auto mb-8 h-5 w-5" />
    <h2 class="text-2xl leading-snug sm:text-4xl">
      Maybe it's not a climate issue. Maybe it's a design issue.
    </h2>
    <p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-2xl text-paper/75">
      Our old cities gave us shade, water, greenery and shelter without trying.
      So why can't our neighbourhoods do the same today? I think we stopped
      designing for the place we actually live in.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
